        <body>
            <div xml:id="d121">

                <pb facs="0354" n="270" xml:id="pg270"/>

                <head>No. 117.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Beach to Mr. Porter.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate-General.</hi><lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Guayaquil, Ecuador</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-07-17"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 17, 1885</date>.
                        (Received August 3.).</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 57.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I again continue the story of Santos from
                    the point of concluding my previous dispatch on the same subject, dated July 10.
                    Immediately after closing that dispatch I sent the following cablegram to
                    Washington: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Guayaquil</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">July</hi> 10.</p>
                        <p rendition="#flush">Secretary <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bayard</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Washington:</hi></p>
                        <p>Responding to my telegram 6th President says Santos included in pardon,
                            and will be liberated after short delay to make necessary decree.</p>
                        <p rendition="#right">BEACH.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>On the 12th I received the following telegram from the President: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p rendition="#center">[Translation.]</p>
                        <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Quito</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">July 12.</hi></p>
                        <p rendition="#flush">Consul-General <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Beach</hi>:</p>
                        <p>This day by mail order sent to governor of Manibi for the liberation of
                            Julio Santos.</p>
                        <p rendition="#right">PRESIDENT.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>On the 13th I sent the following cablegram to Washington: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Guayaquil</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">July</hi> 13, 1885.</p>
                        <p rendition="#flush">Secretary <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bayard</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Washington:</hi></p>
                        <p>President telegraphed yesterday saying ordered by mail to governor Manibi
                            for Santos’s release.</p>
                        <p rendition="#right">BEACH.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>The same day I sent a translation of the following to the President: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Guayaquil</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">July</hi> 13.</p>
                        <p rendition="#flush">Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">President</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Quito:</hi></p>
                        <p>Have cabled to Washington the purport of your two telegrams.</p>
                        <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Consul-General</hi>
                            BEACH.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>On the 11th I wrote to Mr. Santos the import of the President’s telegram to me of
                    the 10th, and expressed it as my belief that he would soon be freed. By due
                    course of mail he should have received this on the 15th.</p>
                <p>The order which the President telegraphed left Quito the 12th. Should by due
                    course of mail reach Mr. Santos today or to-morrow.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>HORATIO N. BEACH,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Consul-General.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
